<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ==================================================================
  ORGANISM — the body-level declaration

  A lamina describes one system.  Nothing inside a lamina can describe
  how it reaches another one, because the topology is not a fact about
  either end: "circulation feeds oxygen delivery" belongs to neither
  the circulatory nor the respiratory file, and putting it in one of
  them would make that lamina un-runnable on its own.  So it lives
  here, one level up, the same way ingestion_manifold.xml holds what
  belongs to no single sensor.

  This file declares two things:

    <laminae>              which laminae make up this body
    <permeability_gates>   where, and under what conditions, a value
                           crosses from one to another

  A gate is a VALVE, not a wire.  It has a controller that decides
  how open it is each cycle, and a transfer stage that decides what
  actually crosses when it is open.  Both are declared, never
  hard-coded — the same rule the laminae follow.

  ==================================================================
  THE FIVE RULES, enforced when this file is parsed (organism.py)

  1. A gate is CLOSED until its controller opens it.  Closed is the
     stable state; a pressure-cooker valve is shut and opens on
     condition, not the other way round.  There is no "start open"
     attribute to write, which is the point.

  2. The SOURCE of a crossing is always a composite, never a bare
     attribute.  A composite is the unit a lamina already reasons
     about — it is what a tag addresses and what carries distribution
     weights — so a gate that reads one is reading something the
     model already says is meaningful together.

  3. The DESTINATION is always an attribute declared INTER_LAMINA.
     Writing into another lamina's SENSOR would forge a measurement;
     writing into its PRELIMINARY would overwrite something it
     computes for itself.  The receiving lamina has to have said "this
     is where somebody else's number attaches" before anybody may
     attach one.

  4. Every controller declares TWO boundaries, and the shut one must
     be strictly under the open one — close_below/open_above for a
     threshold gate, release_below/match_threshold for a key gate.
     One boundary chatters: the feed carries deliberate Gaussian
     noise, so a value sitting on it opens and shuts the gate every
     cycle.  Measured, on a signal parked on the threshold: 177
     transitions in 400 cycles with one boundary, 1 with the band this
     file declares.  A key gate measured 148 flips in 400 the same
     way, which is why the rule covers both rather than just the one
     it was written for.

  5. Any parameter something may LEARN must be declared here with a
     clamp on it.  Nothing in v1 learns — the key vector is static and
     drift is measured before it is corrected — but the rule is
     enforced now, while there is one place to enforce it, rather than
     after the count of learnable parameters has grown.
  ==================================================================
-->
<organism id="bdt_default" version="1.0">

  <name>Default Body</name>
  <description>
    Circulation and respiration, joined at the one seam the two laminae
    already agree on: oxygen delivery needs a cardiac output, and only
    one of them computes it.
  </description>

  <laminae>
    <lamina id="lamina_circulatory" />
    <lamina id="lamina_respiratory" />
  </laminae>

  <!-- ==============================================================
       PERMEABILITY GATES

       pg_co_to_resp is the first live seam.  lamina_respiratory
       declares CO_ref as INTER_LAMINA and says so in its own
       description; this is the other half of that sentence.

       Why the source is pump_state rather than CO: rule 2.  The gate
       reads the composite and <extract> names which of its attributes
       goes on the wire.  Reading the composite is what lets the
       controller watch the state of the pump as a whole while sending
       only the one number the far side declared room for.
       ============================================================== -->
  <permeability_gates>

    <gate id="pg_co_to_resp"
          from="lamina_circulatory:pump_state"
          to="lamina_respiratory:CO_ref"
          controller="threshold">

      <description>
        Cardiac output crossing into oxygen delivery. Held shut while
        the pump is unremarkable, because a respiratory model resting
        on its own declared baseline of 5 L/min is not wrong — it is
        only uninformed, and an uninformed model that is stable beats a
        coupled one that oscillates.

        "Unremarkable" is measured against what the receiver is already
        assuming, and it is two-sided. The first version of this gate
        watched the raw normalised CO and opened above 0.75, which is a
        cardiac output of 9.5 L/min: it opened for a racing heart and
        stayed shut for a failing one. Measured, holding HR at 35:
        cardiac output settles at 3.39 L/min, the seam never opens, and
        respiration goes on computing oxygen delivery from 5.0 — wrong
        by 32%, silently, in the direction a clinician would care about
        most. A seam whose own description says "while the pump is
        unremarkable" has to treat shock as remarkable.
      </description>

      <!-- Which attribute of the source composite goes on the wire. -->
      <extract attribute="CO" />

      <!-- watch names the attribute the controller reads; aggregate
           says how to reduce the composite's normalised vector to the
           one number the thresholds compare against.  attr = just the
           watched attribute; mean and max take the whole vector;
           deviation = the watched attribute's DISTANCE from reference.
           Units are [0,1] — the normalised space — so the same numbers
           mean the same thing whether the attribute is in L/min or
           mmHg.

           reference is the receiver's OWN declared baseline for CO_ref
           (5.0 L/min) expressed in the sender's normalised space:
           (5.0 - 2) / (12 - 2) = 0.30.  That is the point the gate
           should measure departure from, because it is precisely what
           the far side assumes while nothing is crossing.  The gate
           opens when reality stops matching that assumption, either
           way.

           Neither boundary is a taste. open_above comes from the
           RECEIVER's declared tolerance: target_do2 is 1000 +/- 250 and
           DO2 = CaO2 * CO_ref * 10, so at the measured CaO2 of 19.80 the
           CO error that pushes DO2 out of tolerance is 250/198.0 = 1.262
           L/min, which is 0.126 normalised. 0.125 is that number, and it
           means the seam opens exactly when staying shut would put the
           far side's own outcome out of range.

           close_below comes from measured noise. CO_norm on the live
           feed has sd 0.0104; feeding the controller a signal parked on
           open_above, 400 cycles:

             close_below 0.1249 (one boundary)   177 transitions
             close_below 0.115  (0.010 band)      91
             close_below 0.100  (0.025 band)       3
             close_below 0.090  (0.035 band)       1

           0.09 is 3.4 sd of deadband, and the first width where the
           gate stops arguing with the noise. In CO terms: it opens when
           output leaves 5.0 +/- 1.25 L/min and shuts when it returns
           inside 5.0 +/- 0.9. -->
      <controller_params watch="CO" aggregate="deviation" reference="0.30"
                         open_above="0.125" close_below="0.09"
                         refractory_cycles="5" />

      <!-- rate is the EWMA coefficient applied once per cycle of the
           RECEIVING lamina; time_delay is counted in those same
           cycles.

           time_delay is 0 because there is nothing to compensate for:
           the receiver re-derives from a crossing within the same tick
           (measured — see the tick order in organism.py), so a delay
           here would be a delay somebody chose rather than one the
           machinery imposed.  Nobody has a reason to choose one on this
           seam yet.

           That leaves rate carrying the whole anti-oscillation load,
           which is what G4 says it should.  Measured, holding the
           circulation at HR 200 so its own loop makes CO swing:

             rate 1.00   CO_ref 13.20 -> 7.13 -> 14.91 -> 10.62
             rate 0.20   CO_ref  6.64 -> 6.74 ->  8.37 ->  8.82

           0.2 walks the receiver up to the sender instead of handing it
           the sender's argument with itself.

           on_close="hold" keeps the last value that crossed, stamped
           with its age, so the far side sees an old number labelled old
           rather than one snapping back to a baseline nobody chose.
           decay_to_baseline is the other option, for a seam where a
           stale coupled value would be worse than an uninformed one. -->
      <transfer mode="proportional"
                rate="0.2"
                time_delay="0"
                on_close="hold"
                confidence="0.8" />
    </gate>

  </permeability_gates>

</organism>
